Fujiyama Renderer
=================

  Overview
-----------------
Fujiyama is free and open source, distribution oriented ray-tracing renderer
designed to handle production image rendering. 
* Compatibility - written in C++11
* Portability - depends on no external library (x)
* Simplicity - requires only single C++ header file and library
* Compact - the core library is small and easy to embed
* Open Source - licensed under the terms of MIT License (See LICENSE)
   (x) some external tools require OpenEXR and OpneGL/GLUT

  Features
-----------------
* Platforms
  + Linux
  + Mac OSX
  + Windows

* Rendering
  + Multi-threaded rendering - dynamic assignement of tile rendering per theread
  + Rendering preview - fbview shows rendering progress
  + Region rendering - accelerates distribution rendering pipeline
  + Tile based rendering

* Supported Primitives
  + Triangle Mesh - Compact representation
  + Bezier Curves - Unlimited smoothness subdividing in render time 
  + Volume Primitive - Ray traced self-shadowing
  + Point Cloud - Can be rendered as particles

* Procedure Plugin
  + Volume Filling Procedure - Pyroclastic clouds procedure provided
  + Volume Filling Procedure - Spline wisps procedure provided
  + Volume Filling Procedure - Surface wisps procedure provided

* File format
  + Original mipmap format - hdr2mip is provided
  + Original framebuffer format - fb2exr is provided
  + Command-like scene description language - works with text filter tools
  + Python Binding scene description - can utilize control structures and libraries
  + FrameBuffer Viewer - displays *.fb and *.mip 
  + Input format - *.ply, *.obj, *.mip, *.hdr, *.jpg (through Python API)
  + Output format - *.fb, *.exr (through Python API)


* Lighting
  + Point light - traditional point light
  + Grid light - typical area light with single/double-sided option
  + Sphere light - another area light
  + Dome light - supports IBL with environment map

* Animation
  + Camera motion blur - sampling non-linear camera motion
  + Transform motion blur - sampling non-linear object transformation
  + Velocity attribute motion blur - useful for point count
    changing geometry

* Shading and Texture
  + On demand reading of mipmap - unlimited number of texture images
  + Pluggable shader DSO - shaders can be written in C/C++ 
  + Subsurface scattering - implemented in shader
  + Bump mapping - perturb normals based on mipmap images
  + Primitive groups - multiple shader assignment on triangle mesh

* Acceleratotion structure
  + Grid accelerator - for meshes (dense distribution)
  + BVH accelerator - for object instances (sparse distribution)
  + Object instance - saves memory usage

* Sampling and Filtering
  + Gaussian and Box pixel filters - supports filter width bigger than 1 pixel
  + Adaptive grid pixel sampling - subdivides pixels based on color threshold
  + Uniform grid pixel sampling
  + Jittered pixel sampling 

* Extensibility
  + Rendering callbacks - 5 points to hook rendering process

  Transfer-function volume preview (CPU)
------------------------------------------------
A volume can be shaded by a piecewise-linear transfer function for a quick
CPU preview that is independent of the legacy raymarch shaders. Each
TransferKnot maps a density to an extinction coefficient and an RGB emission;
densities between knots are linearly interpolated and values outside the
knot range are clamped to the two end knots. The first call to
Volume::SetTransferFunction builds a sparse density hierarchy over the voxel
grid; changing the knots later only replaces the function and never rescans
or rebuilds the grid, while SetValue refits the affected leaf blocks and
their ancestors in O(log N) node updates. Density intervals that are fully
transparent under the current function are skipped whole.

The example below builds the volume entirely in memory and writes a PPM
image, using the C++17 standard library only:

    #include "fj_volume.h"
    #include "fj_shading.h"
    #include "fj_object_group.h"
    #include "fj_object_instance.h"
    #include "fj_volume_accelerator.h"
    #include "fj_accelerator.h"
    #include <vector>

    using namespace fj;

    Volume volume;
    volume.Resize(64, 64, 64);
    volume.SetBounds(Box(Vector(-1, -1, -1), Vector(1, 1, 1)));
    for (int z = 0; z < 64; z++)
      for (int y = 0; y < 64; y++)
        for (int x = 0; x < 64; x++)
          volume.SetValue(x, y, z, /* density */ 0.f);

    std::vector<TransferKnot> knots;
    knots.push_back(TransferKnot(0.,   0.,  Color(0, 0, 0)));
    knots.push_back(TransferKnot(.15, .8,  Color(.10f, .04f, .02f)));
    knots.push_back(TransferKnot(.45, 2.2, Color(.95f, .35f, .08f)));
    volume.SetTransferFunction(knots);

    ObjectInstance object;
    object.SetVolume(&volume);
    object.ComputeBounds();

    ObjectGroup group;
    group.AddObject(&object);
    group.ComputeBounds();
    const_cast<Accelerator *>(group.GetSurfaceAccelerator())->Build();
    VolumeAccBuild(const_cast<VolumeAccelerator *>(
        group.GetVolumeAccelerator()));

    TraceContext context = SlCameraContext(&group);
    context.transfer_volume = true;   // enable the preview path
    context.raymarch_step = .03;      // world-space step length

    Ray ray;                          // O + t D
    ray.orig = Vector(0, 0, -3);
    ray.dir  = Vector(0, 0, 1);
    ray.tmin = .001;
    ray.tmax = 8;

    Color4 color;
    double t_hit = 0;
    SlTrace(&context, &ray.orig, &ray.dir, ray.tmin, ray.tmax,
        &color, &t_hit);

A complete, compilable version that writes the image to a binary PPM file is
in examples/transfer_volume_preview.cpp. Build and run it from the
repository root after the core library has been compiled:

    c++ -std=c++17 -O1 -I. -Isrc \
        examples/transfer_volume_preview.cpp \
        .task-build/libfujiyama.a -o transfer_volume_preview
    ./transfer_volume_preview preview.ppm

IntegrateTransfer can also be called directly on a Volume for an object-space
integration; VolumeTransferStats reports the hierarchy nodes visited and the
density samples actually taken. With transfer_volume left at its default of
false, every existing render path keeps its original behavior.

